  "answer": "Yes—other elements can neutralise or diminish a problematic connotation, but this is not automatic.\n\nManual practice: The examiner should consider the trade mark as a whole. Other incorporated features can diminish the offending material’s impact and affect how the relevant consumer interprets it (TMM/Part29/3/3/5). The Manual illustrates this with Bavaria, quoting the finding that three references to “Holland” “at least neutralise the connotation with the State of Bavaria” (same passage). For standalone geographical references, the Manual also expressly treats the absence of mitigating elements or surrounding circumstances as one of the criteria for confusion (TMM/Part29/9/9/3~1).\n\nAct: Section 43 requires rejection where use in relation to the particular goods or services would be likely to deceive or cause confusion because of a connotation of the mark or a sign within it (TMA1995/s43). Thus, the Manual’s whole-mark assessment addresses whether that statutory likelihood remains despite the other elements; their presence alone does not establish that the connotation has been neutralised."
